<h1>Pattern matching</h1>

<p>
  Pattern matching is how kex branches. A <code>match</code> expression lists
  <em>arms</em> — a shape on the left, a result on the right — and the first
  arm whose shape fits is the one that runs.
</p>

<Code code={`match 2 do
  1 -> "one"
  2 -> "two"
  _ -> "many"
end`} repl />

<p>
  <code>_</code> is the wildcard: it matches anything, so it works as a
  catch-all. Arms are tried top to bottom, so put specific cases before
  general ones.
</p>

<p>
  Patterns do more than compare values — they <strong>destructure</strong>
  them. Here a tuple is unpacked into <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> in a
  single step, no indexing or temporary variables.
</p>

<Code code={`match (3, 4) do
  (a, b) -> a + b
end`} repl />

<p>
  This is also the honest way to handle an Optional: instead of papering over
  the missing case with a single default, you spell out both.
</p>

<Code code={`match Just(5) do
  Just(x) -> x
  None -> 0
end`} repl />

<Code code={`match None do
  Just(x) -> x
  None -> 0
end`} repl />

<p>
  Patterns aren't limited to <code>match</code> — a function can be defined in
  multiple <strong>clauses</strong>, each matching a different shape of
  argument. Clauses are tried top to bottom, same as match arms:
</p>

<Code code={`let factorial(0) = 1
let factorial(n) = n * factorial(n - 1)
factorial(5)`} repl />

<p>
  A trailing <code>when</code> narrows a clause with an extra condition — the
  arm only fires if the guard holds too:
</p>

<Code code={`match 7 do
  x when x < 0 -> "negative"
  0 -> "zero"
  _ -> "positive"
end`} repl />
